 @tsunamie1015  DLAA is the same thing as DLSS. Render resolution is a separate setting you send to the driver. Just silly Nvidia or game devs named it weird to begin with. They're putting it properly in the scaling options now where you click through Performance, Balanced, Quality and then DLAA. Still, this is completely irrelevant fluff, the point was having DLSS, and thus DLAA in every game at your disposal is way more convenient (no optiscaler needed) and even better quality than the current state of FSR4.

There's no AA method that works better, so either way you would be trying to use FSR4 or DLSS in some fashion. TAA is not great but its a hell of a lot better than the older alternatives when it comes to this level of detail in games we've gotten since like 10 years ago. There's just no reason to use it anymore when we have DLSS or FSR4.

There's no way I'm playing games like Indiana Jones without setting everything to max. Path tracing is like 99% of the reason I even played that game. The cards can run them, just drop the render resolution and be more optimal with your hardware. Settings are more important than render resolution, especially when you use the proper upscalers.

